# REELHOUSE — V1.0 Release Completeness & Release Preparation Specification

**Document Version:** 1.0  
**Status:** Final — V1.0 Scope Lock  
**Product:** REELHOUSE — Personal Digital Cinema  
**Phase:** Release Completeness  
**Scope:** RC.1–RC.7

---

## 1. Purpose

This document defines the **final set of work required to make REELHOUSE complete and ready for its V1.0 release**.

The core REELHOUSE cinema experience, library architecture, metadata system, collections, playback workflow, and offline-media system are considered feature-complete.

The work defined here is therefore **not a reopening of feature development**.

It exists to provide the final application-level capabilities required for a real user-facing release:

- first-run onboarding
- library portability
- TMDB configuration
- feedback and suggestions
- application information and attribution
- polished application launch
- update discovery and release communication

Once RC.1–RC.7 are completed and accepted, **feature development for V1.0 is permanently frozen**.

The project then moves into:

```text
Release Completeness
        ↓
Final Feature Freeze
        ↓
Production Readiness
        ↓
Release Candidate
        ↓
REELHOUSE v1.0
2. Product Principle
REELHOUSE V1.0 must feel like a finished personal application, not merely a technically complete media-management tool.
The final release must support the complete user lifecycle:
Install
  ↓
Welcome
  ↓
Create local profile
  ↓
Configure TMDB
  ↓
Create or restore library
  ↓
Use REELHOUSE
  ↓
Report problems / suggest improvements
  ↓
Receive future updates
  ↓
Continue using REELHOUSE
REELHOUSE remains:
- local-first
- privacy-conscious
- account-free
- standalone
- user-controlled
- offline-capable where designed
- free from unnecessary cloud dependencies
3. Scope Boundary
3.1 In Scope
The following seven release-completeness milestones are locked:
ID	Milestone	Purpose
RC.1	Library Export / Import	Data portability and restoration
RC.2	First-Run Onboarding	Proper first-launch experience
RC.3	TMDB Setup Experience	Make metadata configuration accessible
RC.4	Feedback & Suggestions	Give users a way to report problems and ideas
RC.5	About & Credits	Identity, attribution, version and licensing
RC.6	Launch Experience	Premium application opening experience
RC.7	Update Checking & Release Notes	Close the support → fix → update loop


3.2 Explicitly Out of Scope
These milestones must not become an excuse to introduce additional V1.0 product features.
Do not add:
- social accounts
- cloud accounts
- cloud library synchronization
- streaming
- recommendation engines
- additional media-management functionality
- built-in media playback
- unnecessary analytics
- subscription systems
- social profiles
- messaging systems
- feature-request voting systems
- elaborate customer-support infrastructure
If a new idea appears during implementation, it belongs in the post-V1.0 backlog, unless it is strictly necessary to satisfy an explicit requirement in this document.
4. RC.1 — Library Export & Import
4.1 Objective
Allow a REELHOUSE user to create a portable backup of their library data and restore that data on another REELHOUSE installation.
This is a data-portability feature, not a media-copying feature.
4.2 Export
The user must be able to initiate:
Settings → Library → Export Library
The export should contain the logical REELHOUSE library and relevant user state.
Exportable Information
At minimum:
- canonical movie identities
- canonical TV-show identities
- seasons
- episodes
- detected/discovery identity where required
- TMDB/provider associations
- metadata required by the application
- collections
- collection membership
- watch state
- playback history
- relevant user preferences/settings
- profile information
- profile picture where practical
- export format version
- application version
- schema/data version
Media Files
Media files are NOT exported.
The export must not become a ZIP containing the user's movies and TV shows.
The distinction is:
Library Export
    = catalogue + metadata + user state

Offline Media
    = physical media files
4.3 Secrets
Sensitive credentials must not be blindly included in an export.
In particular:
- TMDB API credentials must not be exported as ordinary library data.
- Authentication secrets must not be exposed through the export.
The import process may therefore require the user to configure TMDB again on the new installation.
4.4 Import
The user must be able to access:
Settings → Library → Import Library
and also:
First-run onboarding → Import Existing Library
The import process must:
1. Select an export file.
2. Validate the file.
3. Validate the export version.
4. Inspect its contents.
5. Show the user what will be imported.
6. Perform the import.
7. Reconcile the imported data with the local installation.
8. Report completion or errors clearly.
Example:
Import Library

Movies              184
TV Shows             37
Collections           12
Watch History         96

[ Import Library ]
[ Cancel ]
4.5 Physical Media Reconciliation
An imported library does not guarantee that the corresponding physical media exists on the new device.
Therefore:
- logical catalogue records remain valid
- unavailable media remain unavailable
- existing local media sources may be reconciled where appropriate
- absolute paths must not be blindly trusted as portable paths
- device-local offline copies must not be assumed to exist merely because they existed on another installation
4.6 Acceptance Criteria
RC.1 is complete when:
- a library can be exported
- exported data can be validated
- a clean installation can import it
- logical identities are preserved
- collections are preserved
- watch state is preserved
- playback history is preserved where supported
- profile information is preserved where supported
- physical media is never falsely represented as available
- secrets are not exposed
- incompatible or corrupt exports fail safely
- import does not create duplicate logical media
5. RC.2 — First-Run Onboarding
5.1 Objective
Provide a polished onboarding experience the first time REELHOUSE is opened after installation.
The onboarding should introduce the application without turning setup into a complicated wizard.
5.2 First Launch Flow
The initial flow is:
REELHOUSE Launch
       ↓
Welcome
       ↓
Profile
       ↓
TMDB Setup
       ↓
Library Setup
       ↓
REELHOUSE Home
5.3 Welcome Screen
The first screen should establish the identity of the product.
Example:
Welcome to REELHOUSE
Your personal digital cinema.

The screen should feel cinematic and premium rather than like a generic software setup wizard.
Primary action:
Continue
5.4 Local Profile
The user creates a simple local profile.
Required
- Display name
Optional
- Profile picture
No account creation is required.
No email address is required.
No password is required.
No cloud profile is created.
The profile represents the local identity of the REELHOUSE installation.
5.5 TMDB Setup
The next onboarding stage explains the role of TMDB.
Example:
Connect REELHOUSE to TMDB
REELHOUSE uses TMDB to identify your movies and TV shows and retrieve their metadata and artwork.
Add your TMDB API key to get the full library experience.

Actions:
- Get a TMDB API Key
- I Have an API Key
- Skip for Now
The user must be allowed to skip this step.
5.6 Skipping TMDB
Skipping TMDB must not prevent entering REELHOUSE.
Instead, the application clearly communicates that metadata functionality is incomplete until TMDB is configured.
Example:
TMDB isn't configured yet
You can continue using REELHOUSE, but identifying your library and loading metadata will require a TMDB API key.
You can add it anytime from Settings → TMDB.

The application must not pretend that the library is fully configured.
5.7 Existing User / Library Import
The onboarding must support two paths:
                    Library Setup
                         │
              ┌──────────┴──────────┐
              │                     │
         New Library          Existing Library
              │                     │
        Configure storage      Import Library
              │                     │
              └──────────┬──────────┘
                         ↓
                       Home
A returning user can therefore restore an existing REELHOUSE library rather than starting from zero.
5.8 Onboarding Completion
After onboarding:
Onboarding
     ↓
Complete
     ↓
Never show again automatically
Subsequent launches go directly to the normal launch experience and Home.
There must be no repeated onboarding annoyance.
6. RC.3 — TMDB Setup Experience
6.1 Objective
Make TMDB API configuration understandable and significantly easier for a normal user.
The application depends on TMDB for proper metadata identification, so configuration must be treated as an important setup task rather than an obscure technical setting.
6.2 Settings Location
Provide a dedicated:
Settings → TMDB
screen.
It should display either a configured or unconfigured state.
Configured
TMDB

Status
● Connected

API Key
••••••••••••••••

[ Test Connection ]
[ Change API Key ]
Not Configured
TMDB

Status
○ Not Configured

REELHOUSE uses TMDB to identify
your movies and TV shows and load
metadata and artwork.

[ Get a TMDB API Key ]
[ Enter API Key ]
6.3 Get API Key
The onboarding/settings flow should provide a clear action that opens the appropriate official TMDB account/developer page.
The user should not have to independently search the internet to discover how to obtain the key.
The application should explain the basic steps without attempting to reproduce TMDB's own registration system.
6.4 Test Connection
After entering the key:
Test Connection
must provide an explicit result.
Success:
TMDB connection successful.

Failure:
TMDB connection could not be verified. Check your API key and try again.

The application should distinguish configuration errors from temporary network failures where practical.
6.5 Security
The API key:
- is stored locally according to the application's credential-storage approach
- must not appear in library exports
- must not appear in logs
- must not be displayed unnecessarily
- must not be included in feedback submissions automatically
7. RC.4 — Feedback & Suggestions
7.1 Objective
Give users a simple way to communicate with the REELHOUSE project.
Settings should contain:
Feedback & Suggestions
7.2 Feedback Categories
Keep the system lightweight.
Suggested options:
What would you like to send?

○ Report a Bug
○ Suggest an Improvement
○ General Feedback
7.3 Bug Reporting
A bug report should encourage useful information such as:
- description
- what the user expected
- what happened
- optional reproduction steps
Where appropriate, the application can provide non-sensitive diagnostic information such as:
- REELHOUSE version
- platform
- build number
The user should remain in control of what is submitted.
7.4 Support Loop
The feedback system is explicitly connected to the update system:
User
 ↓
Reports Bug
 ↓
Developer Investigates
 ↓
Bug Fixed
 ↓
New REELHOUSE Version
 ↓
Release Notes
 ↓
Update Available
 ↓
User Updates
This is one of the reasons RC.7 is part of V1.0 completeness.
8. RC.5 — About & Credits
8.1 Objective
Provide a proper application identity and attribution area.
Settings should contain:
About REELHOUSE
8.2 Information
At minimum:
- REELHOUSE name
- application version
- build number
- project identity
- credits
- relevant project links
- open-source licenses/acknowledgements
- TMDB attribution
The About section must provide the appropriate TMDB attribution required by the service's terms.
8.3 Design
The About screen should feel like part of the REELHOUSE product rather than a generic settings page.
It can retain the application's cinematic/editorial visual language while remaining simple and information-focused.
9. RC.6 — Launch Experience
9.1 Objective
Give REELHOUSE a distinctive application-opening experience.
9.2 Splash
On application launch:
Dark cinematic environment
        ↓
REELHOUSE identity
        ↓
Subtle animation
        ↓
Home
Target duration:
approximately 1–2 seconds
The splash should be:
- short
- polished
- cinematic
- consistent with the application's visual identity
- responsive
- theme-aware where appropriate
9.3 Important Boundary
The animation must not become a fake loading screen.
Do not artificially delay startup merely to make the animation longer.
The application should proceed as soon as startup is ready.
No sound is required.
The experience should establish REELHOUSE's identity without attempting to imitate Netflix, JioHotstar, or another existing service.
10. RC.7 — Update Checking & Release Notes
10.1 Objective
Allow REELHOUSE to tell users when a newer version is available.
This closes the support lifecycle created by RC.4.
10.2 Settings
The About/Settings area should expose:
Check for Updates
Example:
Updates

Current Version
1.0.0

Status
✓ You're up to date

Last checked
Today

[ Check for Updates ]
10.3 Update Available
When a new release exists:
Update Available

REELHOUSE 1.0.1

Bug fixes & improvements

• Fixed library import issue
• Fixed TV metadata matching
• Improved offline playback reliability

[ Update Now ]
[ Later ]
The exact installation mechanism can remain platform-specific.
The important V1.0 requirement is that REELHOUSE can:
1. determine whether a newer release exists
2. inform the user
3. identify the version
4. communicate what changed
5. provide the appropriate update action
10.4 Release Notes
Every release should have release notes.
This is especially important for bug fixes.
Example:
REELHOUSE 1.0.1
Bug Fixes
- Fixed an issue where some imported TV episodes were not displayed.
- Fixed an offline playback availability issue.
Improvements
- Improved startup reliability.
- Improved library reconciliation.
This allows the feedback loop to become visible to the user.
10.5 Update Architecture
The application should use a small, reliable release-information mechanism rather than downloading application packages merely to determine whether an update exists.
Conceptually:
REELHOUSE
    │
    │ Check
    ▼
Release Information
    │
    ├── Current version
    ├── Latest version
    ├── Release notes
    └── Platform update information
The actual update/install mechanism can differ between Windows, Android and future platforms.
11. Settings Information Architecture
The final Settings structure should provide a coherent home for all V1.0 release-completeness capabilities.
Suggested structure:
SETTINGS

Profile
    Profile
    Display Name
    Profile Picture

Library
    Storage
    Import Library
    Export Library

Metadata
    TMDB
    Metadata Settings

Offline Storage
    Device Storage
    Transfers
    Offline Media

Support
    Feedback & Suggestions
    Check for Updates

About
    About REELHOUSE
    Credits
    Licenses
The exact navigation grouping may be adjusted to match the existing Settings implementation, but the capabilities must remain discoverable.
